 {"id": "DB-GAP-052", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "assignee": "foreman-direct", "created_by": "kara-foreman-direct", "title": "P2 — removeTombstones() was dead on arrival (readFileSync on a bare basename -> ENOENT) and named its output after an arbitrary segment ('<first>-cleaned.jsonl', accumulating '-cleaned' on every run); squash listings were unordered", "reasoning": "Found by the bug-class hunt that followed DB-GAP-049. Three defects in src/git/squash.ts: (1) removeTombstones() built `jsonlFiles` from readdirSync (BASENAMES) and then called fs.readFileSync(jsonlFile) / fs.unlinkSync(jsonlFile) without joining the partition path, so every call threw ENOENT and silently returned {error}. It has no callers in src/, so no live impact — but it is an exported API that would fail for any user who wired it up. (2) The merged output was named after jsonlFiles[0] from an UNSORTED listing, so the name was filesystem-dependent and each repeat run appended another suffix: 0007-cleaned-cleaned-cleaned.jsonl. (3) squashPartition / compactHistory / getCompactionStats / removeTombstones all fed unsorted listings into merging, stats and deletion, so records could come back reordered on a partition whose segments straddle the 9999 boundary. Fixed: every listing uses the shared numeric comparator, removeTombstones joins its paths and names the merged file after the LOWEST numeric segment it replaces (contiguous numbering, idempotent re-runs). Proven by src/git/squash-ordering.test.ts (append-order preservation, numeric output name, idempotency, hostile creation order, compaction across the boundary) — RED against the pre-fix revision: 3 failed / 1 passed with 'ENOENT: no such file or directory, open 9999.jsonl'.", "source": "bug-class hunt after DB-GAP-049 (Bane request: 'go look for bugs like the 1000.jsonl file issue')", "acceptance_criteria": ["removeTombstones returns records in append order with a numeric segment name", "repeat runs are idempotent (no '-cleaned' accumulation)", "compaction/stats listings are numerically ordered"], "commit_hash": "3b4b47e", "created_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00", "completed_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00", "files_changed": ["src/git/squash.ts", "src/git/squash-ordering.test.ts"], "guard_result": "tsc clean; new suite green; RED-proven against pre-fix squash.ts", "ci_result": "pending at closeout (commit 3b4b47e)"}
 {"id": "DB-GAP-053", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "complexity": 2, "title": "P3 — squashPartition() writes the compacted Parquet with no ORDER BY, so row order is engine-determined; ordering by the raw timestamp column is unsafe because timestamps are VARCHAR in mixed ISO shapes", "reasoning": "Surfaced while adding ordering tests around the compaction path. The COPY that produces the compacted Parquet is `COPY (SELECT * FROM records WHERE action NOT IN ('tombstone','forget')) TO ... (FORMAT PARQUET ...)` — no ORDER BY, so row order in the compacted artifact is decided by DuckDB's read_json scan (which reads the file list in the given order, potentially in parallel). For append-order reproducibility we would order by a parsed timestamp, but RETR-003 already documents that chat-archive rows mix formats ('.749Z' vs '.676525+00:00'), so a text ORDER BY on the timestamp column is not chronological. Wanted: either order by try_cast(timestamp AS TIMESTAMP) with a stable tiebreaker (e.g. row order/ingest ordinal), or record an explicit ordering contract for compacted partitions and test it.", "source": "bug-class hunt after DB-GAP-049", "created_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00"}
 {"id": "OPS-011", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "title": "P2 — scheduler-namespace S3 autopush failing on nearly every attempt: 3.1 GiB of LOOSE objects make the bundle exceed PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=30s", "reasoning": "Watchdog scan 2026-09-19T16:06Z (cloudflared tunnel watchdog, adjacent finding). Evidence: duckbrain-http journal 3h = 16 'Push warning ... Command failed: git push --set-upstream s3daily master' (scheduler 14 of them in the last 40 min, off-by-one 1, hermes-memory 1). Root cause measured first-hand in /home/kara/duckbrain/namespaces/scheduler: 'git count-objects -vH' = 3592 loose / 3.11 GiB loose vs 83770 in-pack / 251.90 MiB pack; .git total 3.4G; a bounded manual 'git push s3daily master' did NOT complete in 100s and an earlier attempt did not complete in 120s (rc=124 both), while src/git/autocommit.ts PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=30_000 kills each daemon attempt -> every attempt spawns a fresh git bundle create + pack-objects that runs minutes and is killed. Collateral: 4 concurrent pack-objects in that repo were the top CPU consumers during the scan (load avg 56.50/49.21/38.76 vs GAP-125 gate 12). Remote state is healthy (single refs/heads/master 8000933f, fast-forwardable ancestor of local c4a0c68) so this is NOT a recurrence of OPS-009's duplicate-refspec defect; the mirror merely lags 4 commits because the pushes never finish. A repack sweep IS in flight (transient unit duckbrain-repack-scheduler-one2.service, 'git repack -adf', 554s elapsed at scan; duckbrain-ns-repack.timer next pass 09-20 04:28 local) but .git was still 3.4G / 3592 loose at scan time. Ask: (1) confirm push latency < PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS after the repack completes; (2) durable fix — schedule repack for high-churn namespaces (folds into OPS-010) and/or serialize autopush behind a queue with backoff instead of a full bundle attempt per commit flush (PUSH-001); (3) re-check off-by-one/hermes-memory (1 failure each in the same window). Owner: duckbrain.", "source": null, "created_at": "2026-09-19T16:20:24+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T12:30:55-05:00", "detail": {"fingerprint": "08c2a5fd44cfd9c212b86bc08f6a1276fafefe6bcfdaf4c25cba123fa84e7263"}, "completed_at": "2026-09-19T12:30:55-05:00", "resolution": "Resolved 2026-09-19 by the scheduler namespace repack (3.4GB loose-bloated -> 268MB, 1 pack, fsck clean). Loose objects now 575/~21MB. Post-repack push verified: remote == local (c4a0c689) in 25s wall / 6.7 CPU-s, well inside PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=30s. Root cause of the loose bloat was DB-GAP-049 (rotation freeze); weekly duckbrain-ns-repack.timer keeps it from recurring. Premise of the row is now false by construction."}
+{"id": "PUSH-001", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "title": "P2 — in-daemon S3 autopush is ungated: no pushOnCommit check, no per-namespace minimum interval, no single-flight (measured: 485.8 CPU-s in 150s = 3.24 cores, 99.9% in the duckbrain-http cgroup; 8 full-history bundles in 8 minutes for one namespace, two of them overlapping on the same repo)", "reasoning": "pushNamespaceAsync() (src/git/autocommit.ts:512) pushes after every commit flush with no check of s3.pushOnCommit (config has it false and it is ignored; the comment 2 lines above wrongly claims the hook is inert) and no per-namespace rate limit. Because git-remote-s3 has no server side it builds a FULL-HISTORY bundle per push (git_remote_s3/git.py:52 -> 'git bundle create <file> <ref>', no exclusions): measured 470 CPU-s / 76s wall for namespaces/scheduler, vs 23 CPU-s for an incremental equivalent (20x). Live sampling: 485.8 CPU-s per 150s window, all in the daemon's cgroup, dominant process 'git pack-objects'; journal shows scheduler bundles at 08:34/08:38/08:43/08:48/08:53/08:55/08:56/08:58x5/09:01 with two concurrent packs of different tips on the same repo. Daemon cgroup total 12.5 CPU-hours over 32h uptime (~39% of a core) while the node process itself used only 37 minutes. FIX: honor s3.pushOnCommit + intervalSec, coalesce per namespace (e.g. 30-min floor), single-flight per repo, skip when nothing changed since the last successful push; optionally maintenance.auto=false / gc.auto=0 on namespace repos (104 ad-hoc maintenance runs per 24h compete with the pushes). Trade-off to decide: S3 RPO moves from per-commit to the coalescing floor.", "source": "Bane report 2026-09-19 ('lots of git stuff running often ... more cpu than a normal git push') + foreman measurement", "created_at": "2026-09-19T09:45:39-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T17:44:25+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-09-19T17:44:25+00:00", "resolution": "Fixed by foreman tick 533 (commit 2c31641, feat/native-s3): autopush now honors s3.enabled + s3.pushOnCommit (default = zero pushes), per-namespace intervalSec coalescing floor, single-flight per repo, skip-unchanged vs last successful push; sync exit path shares the gate state. 12 new hermetic gate tests (file:// bare remote + post-receive counter); ops006 push-path tests re-gated via withAutopushEnabled. Gates: tsc 0, prettier clean, 153 files / 1200 tests green."}
